        "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
        "source": "pdf_layout",
        "field": "abstract",
        "raw_text": "We introduce a new class of succinct arguments, that we call elastic. Elastic SNARKs allow the prover to allocate different resources (such as memory and time) depending on the execution environment and the statement to prove. The resulting output is independent of the prover's configuration. To study elastic SNARKs, we extend the stream- ing paradigm of [Block et al., TCC'20]. We provide a definitional frame- work for elastic polynomial interactive oracle proofs for R1CS instances and design a compiler which transforms an elastic PIOP into a prepro- cessing argument system that supports streaming or random access to its inputs. Depending on the configuration, the prover will choose differ- ent trade-offs for time (either linear, or quasilinear) and memory (either linear, or logarithmic). We prove the existence of elastic SNARKS by pre- senting Gemini, a novel FFT-free preprocessing argument. We prove its security and develop a proof-of-concept implementation in Rust based on the arkworks framework. We provide benchmarks for large R1CS instances of tens of billions of gates on a single machine.",
